        'formula'   => '<p><strong>Present value = Future value ÷ (1 + r)<sup>n</sup></strong>, where r is the yearly discount rate and n is the number of years.</p><p>The discount factor is 1 ÷ (1 + r)<sup>n</sup>.</p>',
        'example'   => '<p>$50,000 in 10 years at an 8% discount rate:</p><ul><li>Discount factor: <strong>0.4632</strong></li><li>Present value: <strong>$23,159.67</strong></li><li>Discount: <strong>$26,840.33</strong></li></ul>',
        'sections'  => [
            [ 'Why money today is worth more', '<p>A dollar today can be invested and earn a return, so it is worth more than a dollar later. Present value reverses that growth to express a future amount in today’s money.</p>' ],
            [ 'Choosing a discount rate', '<p>Use the return you could earn on a similar investment, or a rate that reflects risk. A higher rate makes the present value smaller. Try a few rates to see how sensitive the result is.</p>' ],
            [ 'Where it is used', '<p>Comparing a lump sum today against payments later, valuing a bond or a business, and judging whether a settlement offer is fair.</p>' ],
            [ 'Why the discount rate you pick changes everything', '<p>Present value is extremely sensitive to the discount rate, more so than most people expect. $50,000 ten years out is worth about $27,840 today at a 6% rate, but only about $19,277 at a 10% rate — a jump of just 4 percentage points shifts the present value by over $8,500. This is exactly why disagreements over "what rate to use" dominate real-world valuation disputes, not just academic ones.</p>' ],
            [ 'Comparing a lump-sum settlement to a stream of payments', '<p>A common real-world use: deciding between a smaller lump sum now or a larger total paid out over several years — a lottery payout, a lawsuit settlement, or a structured insurance payout. Converting each future payment to its present value, using a realistic discount rate, puts both options in the same today\'s-dollars terms so they can actually be compared, rather than naively comparing a smaller number today to a bigger number spread over years.</p>' ],
            [ 'Present value and inflation are related but different', '<p>Discounting for the return you could earn (present value) and discounting for rising prices (inflation) are two separate concepts that sometimes get blended together. A discount rate that already accounts for expected inflation is called a "nominal" rate; one that strips inflation out is "real." Make sure you know which kind of rate you\'re using, since mixing the two produces a distorted result. See our inflation calculator to isolate the inflation piece specifically.</p>' ],
        ],
        'terms'     => [ [ 'Discount rate', 'The yearly rate used to convert future money into today’s value.' ], [ 'Discount factor', 'The multiplier applied to a future amount to get its present value.' ], [ 'Time value of money', 'The idea that money available now is worth more than the same amount later.' ], [ 'Nominal vs. real rate', 'A nominal rate includes expected inflation; a real rate strips inflation out to reflect true purchasing-power growth.' ] ],
        'faqs'      => [
            [ 'What is present value?', 'It is the value today of money you will receive in the future, after discounting for the return you could earn in the meantime.' ],
