        'formula'   => '<p><strong>Current yield = annual coupon ÷ price.</strong></p><p><strong>Approximate YTM = [coupon + (face − price) ÷ years] ÷ [(face + price) ÷ 2]</strong>. The exact yield to maturity needs iteration, so this is a close estimate.</p>',
        'example'   => '<p>$1,000 face, 5% coupon, $950 price, 10 years:</p><ul><li>Annual coupon: <strong>$50</strong></li><li>Current yield: 50 ÷ 950 = <strong>5.26%</strong></li><li>Approximate YTM: 55 ÷ 975 = <strong>5.64%</strong></li><li>Price: <strong>$50 discount</strong> to face value</li></ul>',
        'sections'  => [
            [ 'Current yield vs yield to maturity', '<p>Current yield only looks at the coupon relative to the price. Yield to maturity also counts the gain (or loss) when the bond returns its face value at maturity, so it is the fuller measure of return if you hold to the end.</p>' ],
            [ 'Prices and rates move opposite', '<p>When market interest rates rise, existing bonds fall in price, and the yield of a bought-at-discount bond rises. When rates fall, prices go up.</p>' ],
            [ 'Risks to consider', '<p>Credit risk (the issuer may not pay), interest-rate risk and inflation risk all matter. Treasury securities have the least credit risk. FINRA publishes plain-language bond guides.</p>' ],
            [ 'Why bond prices and yields move in opposite directions', '<p>This trips up a lot of new investors: when the news says "bond yields are rising," it usually means bond prices are falling, not that bonds suddenly got more generous. A bond\'s coupon payment is fixed once issued, so the only way its effective yield can rise to match new, higher market rates is for its price to drop — a newly discounted price makes that same fixed coupon equal a higher percentage return.</p>' ],
            [ 'Premium bonds vs. discount bonds', '<p>A bond trading above face value (a premium) has a coupon higher than current market rates, so its current yield looks attractive but its yield to maturity is pulled down by the guaranteed loss when it returns only face value at maturity. A discount bond works the opposite way: a lower current yield but a built-in gain at maturity that pulls YTM up. Comparing current yield alone, without checking YTM, can be misleading for either type.</p>' ],
            [ 'How bond duration relates to interest-rate risk', '<p>Bonds with longer maturities are generally more sensitive to interest-rate changes than short-term bonds — a 10-year bond\'s price swings more for the same rate move than a 2-year bond\'s does. This is why some investors "ladder" bonds across several maturities instead of concentrating in one, spreading out both the risk and the reinvestment timing.</p>' ],
        ],
        'terms'     => [ [ 'Coupon', 'The yearly interest a bond pays, as a percent of face value.' ], [ 'Face value', 'The amount repaid at maturity, usually $1,000.' ], [ 'YTM', 'Yield to maturity, the total annualized return if held until it matures.' ], [ 'Duration', 'A measure of how sensitive a bond\'s price is to interest-rate changes; roughly, longer maturities mean higher duration.' ] ],
        'faqs'      => [
            [ 'How do you calculate bond yield?', 'Current yield is the annual coupon divided by the market price. Yield to maturity also includes the gain or loss to face value at maturity.' ],
            [ 'Why is my bond’s yield higher than its coupon?', 'You bought it below face value, so you earn the coupon plus the gain when it matures at face value.' ],
            [ 'Is this YTM exact?', 'It is an approximation. Exact YTM is found by solving the price formula, and usually differs by a few hundredths of a percent.' ],
            [ 'Are bond returns guaranteed?', 'Only if held to maturity and the issuer pays. Prices can fall before then.' ],
            [ 'Why do bond prices fall when interest rates rise?', 'New bonds get issued at the higher current rate, making older, lower-coupon bonds less attractive at face value — their price has to drop so their effective yield matches the new market rate.' ],
            [ 'What is the difference between current yield and YTM?', 'Current yield only measures the coupon against today\'s price. YTM also factors in whether you bought at a discount or premium to face value, giving a fuller picture of your total return if held to maturity.' ],
