        'formula'   => '<p><strong>Value = Monthly amount × [(1 + r)<sup>n</sup> − 1] ÷ r</strong>, where r is the monthly rate (annual rate ÷ 12) and n is the number of months. <strong>Invested = monthly amount × months.</strong></p>',
        'example'   => '<p>$200 a month, 24 months, 25% a year assumed:</p><ul><li>Total invested: <strong>$4,800</strong></li><li>Value: <strong>$6,147</strong></li><li>Profit: <strong>$1,347</strong>, ROI <strong>28.1%</strong></li></ul><p>Try a lower return such as 7% to see a more conservative result for a broad stock fund.</p>',
        'sections'  => [
            [ 'What dollar-cost averaging is', '<p>You invest a fixed amount on a regular schedule regardless of price. You buy more shares when prices are low and fewer when high, which smooths your average cost and removes the pressure of timing the market.</p>' ],
            [ 'DCA vs lump sum', '<p>Historically, investing a lump sum right away has beaten spreading it out more often than not, because markets rise more often than they fall. DCA mainly reduces regret and risk if prices drop right after you invest, and it matches how most people actually earn and save.</p>' ],
            [ 'Use realistic returns', '<p>The default return is deliberately high for a volatile asset. For diversified stock funds, tests at 5% to 8% are more typical. Returns are never guaranteed, and the calculator uses a constant rate while real prices fluctuate.</p>' ],
            [ 'Why DCA feels better than it mathematically performs', '<p>Behavioral research consistently finds investors stick with a DCA plan through downturns better than they stick with a lump-sum investment, even when lump sum comes out ahead on paper more often. The "smoothing" effect of DCA mostly reduces regret and emotional stress, not the expected dollar return — which is a real and valid reason to choose it, just not the reason often assumed.</p>' ],
            [ 'What this model assumes that real markets don\'t', '<p>This calculator applies one constant annual return evenly across every month, but real markets move in unpredictable swings — some months up sharply, others down. A DCA plan actually benefits specifically from that volatility, buying more shares during the down months and fewer during the up months, an effect a constant-rate model cannot fully represent.</p>' ],
            [ 'DCA outside of investing: the same idea applies to debt and savings', '<p>The same "fixed amount, regular schedule" principle that defines DCA also works well for other financial habits, such as automatically paying extra toward a loan every month (see our loan payoff calculator) or automatically funneling a set amount into a high-yield savings account. Removing the decision each month — "should I invest/pay/save this month?" — is often the real behavioral win, regardless of the specific application.</p>' ],
        ],
        'terms'     => [ [ 'Dollar-cost averaging', 'Investing a fixed amount at regular intervals.' ], [ 'Average cost', 'Total spent divided by total units bought.' ], [ 'ROI', 'Profit as a percentage of the amount invested.' ], [ 'Lump-sum investing', 'Investing a full amount all at once, rather than spreading it over several smaller purchases.' ] ],
        'faqs'      => [
            [ 'What is dollar-cost averaging?', 'It is investing the same amount on a regular schedule, such as monthly, regardless of the price.' ],
            [ 'Is DCA better than lump-sum investing?', 'A lump sum has usually done better historically, but DCA lowers the risk of investing everything just before a drop and suits steady monthly savers.' ],
            [ 'Does DCA guarantee profit?', 'No. It does not protect against losses if prices keep falling.' ],
            [ 'What return should I enter?', 'Use a conservative assumption. Treat very high returns as a best case, not a plan.' ],
            [ 'Why do people prefer DCA even if lump sum usually performs better?', 'DCA reduces the emotional difficulty and regret risk of investing everything right before a market drop, and it matches how most people actually receive income — in regular paychecks rather than one large sum.' ],
            [ 'Does this calculator account for market volatility?', 'No. It applies one constant rate evenly across every period, while real markets fluctuate — DCA actually benefits from that real-world volatility in a way this simplified model can\'t fully capture.' ],
        ],
        'sources'   => [ [ 'Investor.gov: Dollar-cost averaging', 'https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/glossary/dollar-cost-averaging' ] ],
        'related'   => [ 'compound-interest-calculator', 'roi-calculator', 'retirement-savings-calculator', 'cagr-calculator', 'risk-assessment-tool' ],
    ],

    'sharpe-ratio-calculator' => [
        'focus'     => 'sharpe ratio calculator',
        'secondary' => [ 'sharpe ratio formula', 'what is a good sharpe ratio', 'risk adjusted return calculator', 'portfolio risk calculator' ],
        'title'     => 'Sharpe Ratio Calculator (2026)',
        'meta'      => 'Free Sharpe ratio calculator: enter portfolio return, risk-free rate and standard deviation to see excess return and a Sharpe ratio rating.',
        'excerpt'   => 'Measure how much return you earn for each unit of risk, using the Sharpe ratio.',
        'answer'    => 'A portfolio returning 12% with a 4.5% risk-free rate and a 15% standard deviation has a Sharpe ratio of 0.5. Its excess return is 7.5%, which the calculator rates as Acceptable.',
        'steps'     => [ 'Enter the portfolio’s return for the period.', 'Enter the risk-free rate, such as a Treasury bill yield.', 'Enter the portfolio’s standard deviation.', 'Read the Sharpe ratio, excess return and rating.' ],
        'formula'   => '<p><strong>Sharpe ratio = (Portfolio return − Risk-free rate) ÷ Standard deviation of the portfolio’s returns.</strong></p>',
        'example'   => '<p>12% return, 4.5% risk-free, 15% standard deviation:</p><ul><li>Excess return: <strong>7.5%</strong></li><li>Sharpe ratio: 7.5 ÷ 15 = <strong>0.500</strong></li></ul><p>Rating scale used here: below 0 poor, under 0.5 below average, 0.5 to 1 acceptable, 1 to 2 good, above 2 excellent.</p>',
        'sections'  => [
            [ 'How to read it', '<p>A higher Sharpe ratio means more return per unit of risk. Comparing the Sharpe ratios of two funds with similar goals shows which delivered returns more efficiently.</p>' ],
            [ 'Where the inputs come from', '<p>Use returns and standard deviation over the same period (for example three years annualized), and a risk-free rate such as the yield on short-term Treasury bills.</p>' ],
            [ 'Limitations', '<p>Standard deviation treats upside and downside swings alike and assumes returns follow a bell curve. Past ratios do not predict the future. The rating bands are rules of thumb, not official standards.</p>' ],
            [ 'Sharpe ratio vs. raw return: why both matter', '<p>A fund returning 20% a year sounds better than one returning 12% — until you see that the 20% fund swings wildly while the 12% fund is far steadier. The Sharpe ratio exists specifically to catch this: it can reveal that the "lower return" fund actually delivered more return per unit of risk taken, which is often the more useful comparison for anyone who can\'t stomach large swings.</p>' ],
            [ 'The Sortino ratio: a related but different measure', '<p>One common criticism of the Sharpe ratio is that it penalizes upside volatility just as much as downside volatility, even though investors generally don\'t mind their investments spiking up. The Sortino ratio addresses this by only counting downside deviation in its denominator. If a fund has mostly big up-swings and few down-swings, its Sortino ratio will typically look more favorable than its Sharpe ratio.</p>' ],
            [ 'Using the Sharpe ratio to compare, not to judge in isolation', '<p>A Sharpe ratio on its own doesn\'t tell you much — "0.8" means little without a frame of reference. Its real value comes from comparing two or more investments measured over the identical time period and against the same risk-free rate. Comparing a Sharpe ratio calculated over 1 year against one calculated over 10 years, for instance, can produce a misleading conclusion even though both numbers look comparable at a glance.</p>' ],
        ],
        'terms'     => [ [ 'Risk-free rate', 'The return on a safe investment such as a Treasury bill.' ], [ 'Standard deviation', 'A measure of how much returns vary around their average.' ], [ 'Excess return', 'Return above the risk-free rate.' ], [ 'Sortino ratio', 'A variation of the Sharpe ratio that only counts downside volatility, not upside swings, as risk.' ] ],
        'faqs'      => [
            [ 'What is a good Sharpe ratio?', 'Many investors treat 1 or above as good and 2 or above as very good, but compare it with similar funds over the same period.' ],
            [ 'How do you calculate the Sharpe ratio?', 'Subtract the risk-free rate from the portfolio return and divide by the portfolio’s standard deviation.' ],
            [ 'Can the Sharpe ratio be negative?', 'Yes, when the portfolio earns less than the risk-free rate.' ],
            [ 'Is a higher Sharpe ratio always better?', 'Usually, but only if the inputs are measured over the same period and the return history is long enough to be meaningful.' ],
            [ 'What is the difference between the Sharpe ratio and the Sortino ratio?', 'The Sharpe ratio treats all volatility, up and down, as risk. The Sortino ratio only counts downside volatility, which some investors consider a more realistic definition of "risk" since upside swings are not usually unwelcome.' ],
            [ 'Can I compare Sharpe ratios across different time periods?', 'Not reliably. A Sharpe ratio calculated over one year and another over ten years can look similar numerically but mean very different things — always compare ratios calculated over the same period length.' ],
        ],
        'sources'   => [ [ 'U.S. Treasury: Interest rate statistics', 'https://home.treasury.gov/policy-issues/financing-the-government/interest-rate-statistics' ] ],
        'related'   => [ 'portfolio-analyzer', 'roi-calculator', 'cagr-calculator', 'bond-yield-calculator', 'options-profit-calculator' ],
    ],

    'options-profit-calculator' => [
        'focus'     => 'options profit calculator',
        'secondary' => [ 'call option calculator', 'put option calculator', 'options break even calculator', 'options p&l calculator' ],
        'title'     => 'Options Profit Calculator (2026)',
        'meta'      => 'Free options profit calculator: enter strike, premium, contracts and a target price to see profit or loss, break-even and max loss at expiry for calls and puts.',
        'excerpt'   => 'Estimate the profit, loss and break-even price of a long call or put held to expiration.',
        'answer'    => 'Buying one call with a $155 strike for a $5 premium makes $500 if the stock is $165 at expiry. The break-even price is $160, the most you can lose is $500, and the return on the premium is 100%.',
        'steps'     => [ 'Choose Call or Put.', 'Enter the current stock price, strike price and premium per share.', 'Enter the number of contracts (each covers 100 shares).', 'Enter a target price at expiry and read profit or loss, break-even, max loss and ROI.' ],
        'formula'   => '<p><strong>Long call value at expiry = max(0, price − strike)</strong>; <strong>long put = max(0, strike − price)</strong>.</p><p><strong>Profit = (value − premium) × 100 × contracts.</strong> Break-even is strike + premium for a call and strike − premium for a put. Max loss is the premium paid × 100 × contracts.</p>',
        'example'   => '<p>One call, strike $155, premium $5, target $165:</p><ul><li>Value at expiry: $165 − $155 = <strong>$10</strong></li><li>Profit: (10 − 5) × 100 = <strong>$500</strong></li><li>Break-even: <strong>$160</strong>; max loss <strong>$500</strong>; ROI <strong>100%</strong></li></ul>',
        'sections'  => [
            [ 'Calls and puts', '<p>A call gains when the stock rises above the strike plus premium. A put gains when it falls below the strike minus premium. Both lose the entire premium if they expire worthless.</p>' ],
            [ 'What this calculator does not model', '<p>It shows the result at expiration only. Before expiry, option prices also depend on time left and volatility, and you can close the position early. It covers long positions, not spreads or short options, whose risk can be much larger.</p>' ],
            [ 'Know the risks', '<p>Options can lose their entire value quickly. Read the Options Clearing Corporation’s “Characteristics and Risks of Standardized Options” before trading, and never risk money you cannot afford to lose.</p>' ],
            [ 'Why options lose value even if the stock doesn\'t move', '<p>This surprises a lot of new options buyers: an option can lose value every single day it\'s held, purely from time passing, even if the underlying stock price stays exactly flat. This is called time decay (theta), and it accelerates as expiration approaches — a long-dated option decays slowly at first, then much faster in its final weeks. This calculator only shows the result at expiry, so it doesn\'t capture how much value could erode along the way.</p>' ],
            [ 'In the money, at the money, out of the money', '<p>A call is "in the money" when the stock price is above the strike, "at the money" when it equals the strike, and "out of the money" when it\'s below. For a put, these are reversed. An option that\'s out of the money at expiration is worthless regardless of how close it came — there\'s no partial credit for "almost" being profitable.</p>' ],
            [ 'Why buying options differs from buying stock', '<p>Unlike owning stock outright, an option has an expiration date and can go to zero even if the underlying company is perfectly healthy — if the stock simply doesn\'t move enough, or moves the wrong direction, before expiry. This asymmetry (limited time, a defined maximum loss, but a hard deadline) is what makes options fundamentally different from simply holding shares, and why they\'re generally considered higher-risk for buyers who don\'t fully understand this mechanic.</p>' ],
        ],
        'terms'     => [ [ 'Strike price', 'The price at which the option can be exercised.' ], [ 'Premium', 'The price paid for the option, per share.' ], [ 'Contract', 'Normally covers 100 shares.' ], [ 'Intrinsic value', 'The amount an option is in the money at expiry.' ], [ 'Time decay (theta)', 'The loss of an option\'s value purely from the passage of time, which accelerates as expiration approaches.' ] ],
        'faqs'      => [
            [ 'How do you calculate options profit?', 'Take the option’s value at expiry, subtract the premium, and multiply by 100 shares times the number of contracts.' ],
            [ 'What is the break-even price of a call?', 'The strike price plus the premium paid.' ],
            [ 'What is the most I can lose buying an option?', 'The premium you paid, times 100 shares per contract.' ],
            [ 'Is this financial advice?', 'No. It is an educational estimate at expiry and ignores commissions, taxes and early exercise.' ],
            [ 'Can an option lose value even if I\'m right about the stock\'s direction?', 'Yes. If the stock doesn\'t move far enough, or fast enough, time decay can erode the option\'s value faster than the price movement adds to it, especially close to expiration.' ],
            [ 'What does "in the money" mean?', 'For a call, it means the stock price is above the strike price. For a put, it means the stock price is below the strike. An option that\'s out of the money at expiration expires worthless.' ],
        ],
        'sources'   => [ [ 'Options Industry Council: Education', 'https://www.optionseducation.org/' ], [ 'Investor.gov: Options', 'https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/glossary/options' ] ],
        'related'   => [ 'roi-calculator', 'break-even-calculator', 'portfolio-analyzer', 'sharpe-ratio-calculator' ],
    ],

    'portfolio-analyzer' => [
        'focus'     => 'portfolio analyzer',
        'secondary' => [ 'portfolio allocation calculator', 'portfolio return calculator', 'asset allocation calculator', 'weighted average return calculator' ],
        'title'     => 'Portfolio Analyzer (2026)',
        'meta'      => 'Free portfolio analyzer: enter your holdings and returns to see total value, each asset’s share of the portfolio and your weighted average return.',
        'excerpt'   => 'See how your money is split across assets and what your overall weighted return is.',
        'answer'    => 'Add each holding’s value and return. For example, $30,000 returning 8%, $15,000 returning 20% and $5,000 returning 4% gives a $50,000 portfolio with a weighted return of 11.2%, and allocations of 60%, 30% and 10%.',
        'steps'     => [ 'Enter the name, current value and return of each holding. Use Add Asset for more rows.', 'Click Analyze Portfolio.', 'Read the total value, weighted return and each holding’s share.' ],
        'formula'   => '<p><strong>Weight = holding value ÷ total value.</strong> <strong>Weighted return = Σ (weight × holding return).</strong></p>',
        'example'   => '<p>Three holdings:</p><ul><li>Fund A: $30,000 at 8% → weight <strong>60%</strong></li><li>Fund B: $15,000 at 20% → weight <strong>30%</strong></li><li>Fund C: $5,000 at 4% → weight <strong>10%</strong></li><li>Total: <strong>$50,000</strong>; weighted return: 0.6×8 + 0.3×20 + 0.1×4 = <strong>11.2%</strong></li></ul>',
        'sections'  => [
            [ 'Why allocation matters', '<p>How your money is spread between stocks, bonds, cash and other assets drives most of your portfolio’s risk. Seeing the percentages makes concentration obvious, such as one holding being half of everything you own.</p>' ],
            [ 'Rebalancing', '<p>Over time winners grow into a larger share. Many investors rebalance yearly, or when an allocation drifts a few points from its target, by selling a little of what has grown and buying what has lagged. Taxes may apply when selling in a taxable account.</p>' ],
            [ 'Diversification', '<p>Spreading across many assets that do not move together can reduce risk without necessarily lowering expected return. Broad index funds are a simple way to do this. Estimate risk-adjusted return with the Sharpe ratio calculator.</p>' ],
            [ 'Why weighted return isn\'t the same as a simple average', '<p>A beginner mistake is averaging the returns of all holdings equally, ignoring their size. A $1,000 holding that gained 50% and a $49,000 holding that gained 5% do not average out to 27.5% for your actual portfolio — the larger holding dominates the real result, which weighted return correctly captures and a simple average does not.</p>' ],
            [ 'Concentration risk: when one holding is quietly too big', '<p>It\'s easy for a single winning stock, or a single employer\'s stock in a 401(k), to grow into a much larger share of a portfolio than originally intended, simply because it outperformed everything else. This tool\'s allocation percentages make that concentration visible — a holding that\'s drifted to 40%+ of the total portfolio deserves a second look, even if it\'s been a great performer so far.</p>' ],
            [ 'How to use this alongside a target allocation', '<p>Many investors set a target split in advance — for example, 70% stocks, 20% bonds, 10% cash — based on their age and risk tolerance. Enter your actual current holdings here periodically and compare the resulting percentages against your target; a gap of more than a few percentage points is usually the trigger point professionals use to rebalance.</p>' ],
        ],
        'terms'     => [ [ 'Asset allocation', 'How a portfolio is divided between asset types.' ], [ 'Weighted return', 'Average return where each holding counts in proportion to its size.' ], [ 'Rebalancing', 'Adjusting holdings back to target percentages.' ], [ 'Concentration risk', 'The added risk of having too large a share of a portfolio in a single holding or asset.' ] ],
        'faqs'      => [
            [ 'How do you calculate a portfolio’s return?', 'Multiply each holding’s return by its share of the total value and add the results.' ],
            [ 'What is a good asset allocation?', 'It depends on your age, goals and comfort with risk. A common rule of thumb is more stocks when you are younger and more bonds closer to retirement, but there is no single right answer.' ],
            [ 'How often should I rebalance?', 'Many people rebalance once a year or when an asset class drifts several percentage points from target.' ],
            [ 'Does this save my data?', 'No. The numbers stay in your browser and are not stored.' ],
            [ 'Why is weighted return different from just averaging my holdings\' returns?', 'Because a simple average treats every holding equally regardless of size. Weighted return correctly gives more influence to your larger holdings, which matches how your actual portfolio performs.' ],
            [ 'What is concentration risk?', 'The risk of having too much of your portfolio tied to one holding, sector, or company — so that a decline in that single position has an outsized effect on your total portfolio, even if everything else is diversified.' ],
        ],
        'sources'   => [ [ 'Investor.gov: Asset allocation', 'https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/glossary/asset-allocation' ] ],
        'related'   => [ 'sharpe-ratio-calculator', 'roi-calculator', 'compound-interest-calculator', 'net-worth-calculator', 'options-profit-calculator', 'risk-assessment-tool' ],
    ],

    'fire-calculator' => [
        'focus'     => 'fire calculator',
        'secondary' => [ 'financial independence calculator', 'retire early calculator', 'fire number calculator', '4 percent rule calculator' ],
        'title'     => 'FIRE Calculator (2026)',
        'meta'      => 'Free FIRE calculator: find your FIRE number, the years until financial independence, your target year and your savings rate from expenses, savings and returns.',
        'excerpt'   => 'Calculate how much you need to retire early and how many years of saving it would take.',
        'answer'    => 'With $50,000 of yearly expenses and a 4% safe withdrawal rate, your FIRE number is $1,250,000. Starting from $100,000 and saving $40,000 a year at a 7% return, you would reach it in about 15 years. Your savings rate in this example is about 44.4%.',
        'steps'     => [ 'Enter your annual expenses and current net worth.', 'Enter how much you save each year.', 'Enter the return you expect and a safe withdrawal rate.', 'Read your FIRE number, years to FIRE, target year and savings rate.' ],
        'formula'   => '<p><strong>FIRE number = Annual expenses ÷ Safe withdrawal rate.</strong></p><p>Years to FIRE: each year the calculator grows the balance by your return and adds your savings, <strong>balance = balance × (1 + r) + savings</strong>, until it reaches the FIRE number. Savings rate = savings ÷ (savings + expenses).</p>',
        'example'   => '<p>$50,000 expenses, $100,000 saved, $40,000 added yearly, 7% return, 4% withdrawal:</p><ul><li>FIRE number: <strong>$1,250,000</strong></li><li>Years to FIRE: <strong>15</strong> (balance about $1,281,000)</li><li>Savings rate: <strong>44.4%</strong></li></ul>',
        'sections'  => [
            [ 'The 4% rule', '<p>The 4% rule comes from research on historical U.S. returns and suggests withdrawing about 4% of your starting portfolio in the first year, then adjusting for inflation, has historically lasted about 30 years. People retiring earlier may use a lower rate such as 3% to 3.5% to be safer.</p>' ],
            [ 'Savings rate is the lever', '<p>Reducing expenses helps twice: it lowers the amount you need and raises the amount you save. Try changing expenses and savings in the calculator to see the effect on your timeline.</p>' ],
            [ 'What it leaves out', '<p>The model uses a constant return and ignores inflation changes, taxes, health insurance before Medicare, and market crashes early in retirement. Treat it as a planning estimate and review it every year. Also see our retirement savings calculator.</p>' ],
            [ 'Lean FIRE, Fat FIRE and Coast FIRE: the main variations', '<p>"Lean FIRE" targets a bare-bones, minimal-expense lifestyle and the lowest possible FIRE number. "Fat FIRE" targets a more comfortable or even lavish retirement budget, requiring a much larger number. "Coast FIRE" is different again — it\'s the amount where your current investments, left alone with no further contributions, will compound to a full retirement number by a traditional retirement age, letting you stop aggressively saving (though you still need to cover current expenses). Which variation fits depends entirely on your target lifestyle, not just the math.</p>' ],
            [ 'Why sequence of returns risk matters more for early retirees', '<p>A market crash in the first few years after you stop working does far more damage to a portfolio than the same crash hitting 15 years into retirement, because you\'re withdrawing from a shrunken balance before it has time to recover — this is called sequence of returns risk. Early retirees face more years of this exposure than traditional retirees, which is part of why many FIRE planners deliberately use a more conservative withdrawal rate (3% to 3.5%) than the traditional 4% rule.</p>' ],
            [ 'Healthcare: the expense this calculator doesn\'t model', '<p>For U.S. retirees under 65, healthcare coverage before Medicare eligibility is one of the largest and most unpredictable costs in an early-retirement budget — marketplace premiums, deductibles and out-of-pocket maximums can add thousands of dollars a year that this simplified expense input doesn\'t separately account for. Research your realistic pre-Medicare healthcare costs specifically before finalizing an early-retirement expense estimate.</p>' ],
        ],
        'terms'     => [ [ 'FIRE', 'Financial independence, retire early.' ], [ 'Safe withdrawal rate', 'The share of your portfolio you can withdraw yearly with low risk of running out.' ], [ 'Savings rate', 'Share of income you save.' ], [ 'Sequence of returns risk', 'The added danger of a market downturn striking early in retirement, before the portfolio has time to recover, compared to the same downturn happening later.' ], [ 'Coast FIRE', 'The point at which current investments alone, without further contributions, will grow to a full retirement number by a traditional retirement age.' ] ],
        'faqs'      => [
            [ 'How do you calculate your FIRE number?', 'Divide your yearly expenses by your safe withdrawal rate. At 4%, multiply expenses by 25.' ],
            [ 'Is the 4% rule safe?', 'It held up in historical U.S. data over 30-year periods, but it is not a guarantee, and longer retirements may need a lower rate.' ],
            [ 'How long does it take to reach FIRE?', 'It depends mostly on your savings rate. In the example, saving about 44% of income takes about 15 years.' ],
            [ 'Should I count my home?', 'Usually not unless you plan to sell it. Use investable assets.' ],
            [ 'What is the difference between Lean FIRE, Fat FIRE and Coast FIRE?', 'Lean FIRE targets a minimal-expense lifestyle (a lower FIRE number); Fat FIRE targets a more comfortable budget (a higher number); Coast FIRE is the point where current savings alone will compound to full retirement by a traditional age without further contributions.' ],
            [ 'Does this calculator account for healthcare costs before Medicare?', 'Only if you include them in your annual expenses input. For U.S. retirees under 65, pre-Medicare healthcare is often one of the largest and least predictable costs in an early-retirement budget, so research your specific expected costs separately.' ],
        ],
        'sources'   => [ [ 'Investor.gov: Save and invest', 'https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/save-and-invest' ] ],
        'related'   => [ 'retirement-savings-calculator', '401k-calculator', 'compound-interest-calculator', 'net-worth-calculator', 'risk-assessment-tool' ],
    ],

    ];
}
